              },
              "children": [
                {
                  "id": "lede",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "A reliable semi truck battery is the backbone of your truck's electrical performance. From powering your starter to supporting lights, HVAC, sensors, and modern onboard electronics, your battery does far more than most drivers realize.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full",
                    "anchor": "post-lede"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-intro-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "But when a battery begins to fail, the warning signs can be subtle, and catching them early is the difference between a smooth route and an unexpected roadside breakdown.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-intro-2",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "At Sun State International, we help Florida drivers and fleets stay ahead of costly downtime by offering professional diagnostics, high-quality semi truck battery replacement, and one of the region's largest inventories of parts for International® trucks. We'll walk through the most common red flags of a failing battery and how to protect your truck's electrical system long-term.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-why-it-matters",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Why Your Semi Truck Battery Matters More Than You Think",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "why-it-matters"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-why-it-matters-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Modern trucks rely heavily on their electrical systems. Between emissions controls, telematics, A/C, and sleeper cab electronics, your battery is responsible for powering more components than ever. A weak battery doesn't just make cold starts harder; it puts strain on your alternator, reduces fuel efficiency, and can even trigger electrical faults.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "fig-1",
                  "type": "image",
                  "props": {
                    "width": "full",
                    "image": {
                      "src": "/img/blog/signs-its-time-to-replace-your-semi-truck-battery/1.jpg",
                      "width": 1280,
                      "height": 1024,
                      "alt": "Close-up of corroded semi truck battery terminals and wiring during an electrical inspection"
                    }
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h-top-signs",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Top Signs Your Semi Truck Battery Is Failing",
                    "headingLevel": 2,
                    "align": "left",
                    "anchor": "top-signs"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-1",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Let's take a closer look at the most common indicators your battery is nearing the end of its life. If you notice any of these symptoms, it's time to get your truck inspected by a certified technician.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-top-signs-2",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Slow or Difficult Starts",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-3",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If your truck hesitates or cranks slowly before turning over, the semi truck battery may be losing its charge capacity. While the starter and alternator should also be inspected, truck starting problems are one of the first clues that replacement is on the horizon.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-top-signs-4",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Dimming Lights or Weak Electrical Functions",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-5",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Fading headlights, interior lights that flicker, or malfunctioning gauges often point to a weakening battery. Because today's semi truck electrical systems are highly sensitive, low voltage can trigger incorrect readings or intermittent faults.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-top-signs-6",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Frequent Jump Starts",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-7",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If you find yourself jump-starting your truck more often than usual, that's a clear sign the battery can no longer hold a reliable charge. Even one jump-start can indicate underlying deterioration depending on the battery's age.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-top-signs-8",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Battery Case Swelling or Corrosion",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-9",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Heat, vibration, and heavy loads can physically damage a battery over time. Keep an eye out for:",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "list-top-signs-10",
                  "type": "prose-list",
                  "props": {
                    "ordered": false,
                    "items": [
                      {
                        "text": "Bulging sides"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Leaking electrolyte"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Heavy corrosion around terminals"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": "Loose or damaged cables"
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-11",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "These signs warrant immediate attention and, likely, a semi truck battery replacement.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-top-signs-12",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Older Battery Age (3–5 Years)",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-13",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Most semi truck batteries last 3–5 years, depending on usage, idling habits, and climate. Florida's heat accelerates battery wear, which means owner-operators should begin testing their batteries after the three-year mark.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "h3-top-signs-14",
                  "type": "heading",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "Electrical System Warning Lights",
                    "headingLevel": 3,
                    "align": "left"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-15",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If your dash displays voltage warnings, alternator alerts, or electrical system faults, the battery may not be providing consistent power. Though not always battery-related, these alerts should never be ignored.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "p-top-signs-16",
                  "type": "text",
                  "props": {
                    "text": "If your truck shows one or more of these signs, it's time to schedule a professional inspection. Avoiding replacement can put unnecessary strain on your alternator and shorten the lifespan of other electrical components, turning a simple battery replacement into a far more expensive repair.",
                    "align": "left",
                    "width": "full"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "id": "co-top-signs-17-row",
                  "type": "row",
                  "props": {
                    "gap": 0
                  },
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "id": "co-top-signs-17",
                      "type": "column",
                      "props": {
                        "span": 12
                      },
                      "styles": {
                        "base": {
